<ContentPage
  title="On AI — CodeXX DTDK"
  description="How CodeXX DTDK uses LLMs: extensively, but under human review, test-driven development, dogfooding, and enforced best practices. We are engineers using modern tools — not vibe-coders."
  eyebrow="On AI"
  heading="Yes, we use LLMs. Here's how — and how we don't."
  lead="We're upfront about leaning on AI. We're just as upfront that every line of it lands inside a serious engineering process."
  active="ai"
>
  <p>
    The honest reason is scale. The human and economic resources behind CodeXX
    are small; the surface area of the problem — a legacy-free engine for
    understanding C++ and a whole ecosystem of tools on top of it — is not. So we
    use <strong>LLMs extensively</strong> throughout development to close that gap.
  </p>

  <p>
    What we want to be equally clear about is that using AI heavily and being
    careless are not the same thing. Here is what actually surrounds every AI
    contribution:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Human review, always.</strong> All code is thoroughly reviewed by
      humans before it lands. Nothing merges because a model was confident about it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Test-driven development.</strong> Behaviour is pinned down by tests,
      not by vibes — which is exactly what keeps AI-assisted changes honest.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We dogfood our own tools.</strong> We self-host the engine and run
      it across the entire corpus of our own codebase and all of its
      dependencies. If the tools aren't reliable on our own code, we find out
      before you do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Best practices are enforced by automation.</strong> Pre-commit
      hooks, CI actions, formatters, and checks gate the work — the standard
      doesn't depend on anyone remembering to uphold it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Strong LLM guidelines.</strong> We hold the models to explicit
      guidelines for workflows and artifact quality, so AI output meets the same
      bar as anything else in the repository.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="pull">
    We are not vibe-coders shipping code we don't understand.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is serious engineering that <strong>leverages the most modern tools to
    reach the best possible result</strong>. AI is an accelerator here — pointed
    at a real architecture, held to real tests, and reviewed by real engineers who
    know exactly what the code does and why.
  </p>

  <h2>A reality check</h2>

  <p>
    We should also be honest in the other direction. AI coding agents are getting
    better every day; they now produce high-quality code at a speed that, like it
    or not, a human developer simply can't match. But they will
    <strong>betray you when you're most vulnerable</strong> — hallucinating
    something plausible and wrong exactly when you least expect it. That is
    precisely why we never skip a review before merging, and why we never let an
    LLM drive design. They are confined to <strong>implementation and
    counsel</strong>, nothing more.
  </p>

  <p>
    It remains fully <strong>our</strong> responsibility to keep the code
    maintainable, to hold a clear and complete picture of the software — no small
    thing in a codebase this size — and, ultimately, to build a piece of
    software's most important feature: <strong>its soul</strong>.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
